# Compilation targets

All backends consume **IR ELM** (never the source AST directly).

| Target | Role |
|---|---|
| **ELM text** | Pretty-print IR as `elm(...)` and `1` only |
| **Native binary** | Lower IR ELM through **LLVM** to a machine-code executable |
| **.NET CLI** | Emit a **.NET CLI DLL/executable**; **may use** .NET math |
| **Java bytecode** | Emit a **Java class**; **may use** Java math |
| **Interpreter** | Evaluate IR ELM in-process (same executable) |

Native LLVM lowering should realize `elm(x, y) = exp(x) - ln(y)` (complex). .NET and Java backends may call the host language’s `exp` / `log` (and other math) instead of expanding everything to a single opcode.

`elm compile --format` maps: `cli` → .NET DLL, `bytecode` → Java, `binary` → LLVM native. Output `*.elm` is ELM text. See `elm-cli`.

